1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
Look at any 3D protein structure in the Protein Data Bank or

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,680
AlphaFold Database, and you are looking at a single frozen snapshot—as if

3
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:11,520
the molecule were flash-frozen at absolute zero.

4
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:15,360
But inside a living cell, or inside a 65-degree industrial bioreactor,

5
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:19,200
proteins are constantly breathing! Nearly half of all high-value enzyme binding sites

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,040
are what structural biologists call cryptic pockets:

7
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:26,880
cavities whose helical lids look shut tight in a static snapshot,

8
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:30,720
yet thermally swing open and closed in water to swallow a bulky

9
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:34,560
substrate. How can we discover those breathing pockets across 2.34 million proteins

10
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:38,400
without storing terabytes of Molecular Dynamics videos?

11
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:42,181
If you simply search for flexible regions on a protein's surface,

12
00:00:42,181 --> 00:00:45,963
you walk straight into a thermodynamic trap.

13
00:00:45,963 --> 00:00:49,744
Not all motion is useful! A floppy,

14
00:00:49,744 --> 00:00:53,525
unstructured surface loop wiggles through dozens of random shapes,

15
00:00:53,525 --> 00:00:57,307
which means locking it around a substrate molecule costs more than 3.8

16
00:00:57,307 --> 00:01:01,088
kilocalories per mole in conformational entropy—so the substrate never binds.

17
00:01:01,088 --> 00:01:04,869
Meanwhile, storing a dense 300-by-20 probability matrix for every protein takes 24,000

18
00:01:04,869 --> 00:01:08,651
bytes per row, smashing past PostgreSQL's 2-kilobyte threshold and slicing your table

19
00:01:08,651 --> 00:01:12,432
into 12 slow out-of-line disk chunks per protein.

20
00:01:12,432 --> 00:01:17,477
Here is the physical heuristic from ProtProfileMD and FrustraMPNN that solves this.

21
00:01:17,477 --> 00:01:22,523
Instead of tracking raw XYZ coordinates across thousands of frames,

22
00:01:22,523 --> 00:01:27,568
we project each amino acid onto Foldseek's 20 tertiary interaction states—called the

23
00:01:27,568 --> 00:01:32,613
3Di alphabet—and measure its normalized Shannon entropy H-sub-3Di from zero to one.

24
00:01:32,613 --> 00:01:37,659
Look at the three clean physical regimes that emerge:

25
00:01:37,659 --> 00:01:42,704
a rigid catalytic triad locks into a single dominant 3Di state with

26
00:01:42,704 --> 00:01:47,749
entropy near 0.19. A floppy disordered tail scatters across all 20 states

27
00:01:47,749 --> 00:01:52,795
with entropy above 0.70. And right in the middle—between 0.29 and 0.70—sits

28
00:01:52,795 --> 00:01:57,840
the Goldilocks cryptic breathing lid, toggling cleanly between just two bistable hinge states!

29
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:03,147
Watch our 3D comparison stage on the Lake Magadi soda-lake archaeon D3SWG7.

30
00:02:03,147 --> 00:02:08,455
Beneath the surface, the catalytic serine-histidine-aspartate triad sits locked at 0.44 Angstroms

31
00:02:08,455 --> 00:02:13,762
with an opening barrier of just 0.16 kilocalories per mole.

32
00:02:13,762 --> 00:02:19,070
Directly above it, residues 142 through 160 form a cryptic helical lid

33
00:02:19,070 --> 00:02:24,377
with a 3Di entropy of 0.5142 and a breathing score of 0.9948—requiring

34
00:02:24,377 --> 00:02:29,685
only 1.29 kilocalories per mole of thermal energy to swing open and

35
00:02:29,685 --> 00:02:34,992
admit zero-aromatic perfluoroalkyl chains across 2,608 eight-dimensional GiST pocket matches!

36
00:02:34,992 --> 00:02:39,161
Finally, look at what happens under the hood in PostgreSQL 18.

37
00:02:39,161 --> 00:02:43,330
Because even a cryptic hinge only needs its top two 3Di states,

38
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:47,498
their probabilities, and its entropy byte,

39
00:02:47,498 --> 00:02:51,667
our PGFP binary container packs a 300-amino-acid protein into just 1,232 bytes—19.5

40
00:02:51,667 --> 00:02:55,836
times smaller than a dense matrix,

41
00:02:55,836 --> 00:03:00,005
living 100 percent inline on the 8-kilobyte heap page with zero TOAST

42
00:03:00,005 --> 00:03:04,174
overhead. Better still, the 32-byte header embeds a 128-bit balanced 64-hot structural

43
00:03:04,174 --> 00:03:08,342
sketch, letting PostgreSQL compare two 3D folds in two CPU instructions—XOR and

44
00:03:08,342 --> 00:03:12,511
POPCNT—at 58.4 million comparisons per second!

45
00:03:12,511 --> 00:03:16,680
Explore these breathing pockets in 3D right now at ideia dot me.
